`/users` and `/health` can be hammered freely. Apply a global limiter and stricter limits on sensitive endpoints:

```js
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');
app.use(rateLimit({ windowMs: 60_000, max: 120, standardHeaders: true, legacyHeaders: false }));
```

---

### 7. No body size limits (DoS — OWASP A04)

When you add `express.json()`, set a limit:

```js
app.use(express.json({ limit: '100kb' }));
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false, limit: '100kb' }));
```

---

### 8. No error handling / leaks via default Express errors (OWASP A09: Logging Failures, A05)

There's no `try/catch`, no async error wrapper, and no error-handling middleware. Default Express errors can leak stack traces in dev mode if `NODE_ENV` isn't set. Add:

```js
// 404
app.use((req, res) => res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' }));

// Centralized error handler — must have 4 args
app.use((err, req, res, _next) => {
  // Use a real logger; never log secrets/tokens/PII
  console.error({ msg: 'unhandled', err: err.message, path: req.path });
  res.status(err.status || 500).json({
    error: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production' ? 'Internal error' : err.message,
  });
});
```

Also handle process-level failures so the app fails closed:

```js
process.on('unhandledRejection', (e) => { console.error(e); process.exit(1); });
process.on('uncaughtException',  (e) => { console.error(e); process.exit(1); });
```

